                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "e7a0ebbc-b2be-4f56-b6b4-c00cd9a20777",
                        "source_entry_id": "87ccc4c9-01b3-30a4-9986-33b65d1612d1",
                        "source_sense_id": "8481890d-edcc-3de2-8d56-6d59c94f342b",
                        "target_lang_word": "remora",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "remora",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "36b6a3d5-7060-3f23-8bc2-0954184b3631",
                                        "entry_id": "d767bf05-f129-368c-b975-1a16ed319518",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any of various elongate fish from the family Echeneidae, the dorsal fin of which is in the form of a suction disc that can take a firm hold against the skin of larger marine animals."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f10dd072-eb54-4518-993d-3f2ee7dfdcc1",
                                                "sense_id": "36b6a3d5-7060-3f23-8bc2-0954184b3631",
                                                "text": "The Remoræ (Echeneisidæ) form the last family of the soft-finned, subbrachian fishes. They are characterized at once by the top of their heads being flattened, and furnished with transverse series of cartilaginous plates, somewhat similar to the plates under the toes of the Gecko, by which these fish attach themselves to ships, rocks, and marine bodies."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8177c066-f36d-43bd-a45a-38d65a001522",
                                                "sense_id": "36b6a3d5-7060-3f23-8bc2-0954184b3631",
                                                "text": "The remora, lump-sucker, and others are provided with a muscular disk in the form of a sucker, by which they adhere to other fish or bodies moving through the water: [...]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "596aa474-9635-4f5d-ab9a-93dee2eeecc9",
                                                "sense_id": "36b6a3d5-7060-3f23-8bc2-0954184b3631",
                                                "text": "[...] I could not but notice, with some degree of curiosity, the gradual approaches of one of these remorae of society into the good graces of as genuine an English family as ever left the fat fields of Suffolk to pay for peeping at foreign novelty."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "96e070fa-50a1-47a5-9000-b26cb26572d0",
                                                "sense_id": "36b6a3d5-7060-3f23-8bc2-0954184b3631",
                                                "text": "It was all genuine footage of various kinds of ray-fish: first a manta gliding just below the surface of a purple sea near the coast of Argentina. He glided there amongst the light beams that penetrated the water like daggers stabbing into it, while remorae skirted among his wing-fins."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d233c0ce-b242-440f-b589-01d141ed8de7",
                                                "sense_id": "36b6a3d5-7060-3f23-8bc2-0954184b3631",
                                                "text": "The tenacity with which remoras attach to their hosts is best illustrated by the practice of sea turtle fishing by fishermen in the Caribbean and off China and northern Australia [...], and in Yemen and Kenya, where it continues to this day. A fisherman ties a line around the tail of a remora and throws the fish into the water. The remora tightly attaches itself to a turtle, and the remora and its \"catch\" are then hauled ashore."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "82cd9570-37fc-3881-9a9a-b2d7892a80cc",
                                        "entry_id": "d767bf05-f129-368c-b975-1a16ed319518",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A serpent."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "13b59975-cd99-4186-a792-0249b1e264b5",
                                                "sense_id": "82cd9570-37fc-3881-9a9a-b2d7892a80cc",
                                                "text": "LORD SCARSDALE. [...] On the dexter ſide, the figure of Prudence repreſented by a woman, habited argent, mantled azure, holding in her ſiniſter hand a javelin entwined with a remora proper; [...]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6340fcd6-dbe2-3c96-9295-45a04be167d9",
                                        "entry_id": "d767bf05-f129-368c-b975-1a16ed319518",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A delay; a hindrance, an obstacle."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2c95b0f8-0ee0-49d5-9931-39fe1de968d5",
                                                "sense_id": "6340fcd6-dbe2-3c96-9295-45a04be167d9",
                                                "text": "For to ſay [...] That the ſolidneſſe of the Earth is for the ſtation and Manſion of liuing creatures: and the like, is well inquired & collected in METAPHISICKE, but in PHISICKE they are impertinent. Nay, they are indeed but Remoraes and hinderances to ſtay and ſlugge the Shippe from furder ſayling, and haue brought this to paſſe, that the ſearch of the Phiſicall Cauſes hath beene neglected."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c4ba1588-1c08-488e-b074-8a47e47bca0b",
                                                "sense_id": "6340fcd6-dbe2-3c96-9295-45a04be167d9",
                                                "text": "[H]is Majeſty, long ſince weary of the yoke of all Parliaments, (the only Remora to his abſolute intended Monarchy) and repenting of the Act for continuing this, [...] is now reſolved (in proſecution of his priſtine Counſels) by force or policy to diſſolve this Parliament in diſcontent, [...]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1e62761d-b1d2-4ef7-a4fc-63da065310c8",
                                                "sense_id": "6340fcd6-dbe2-3c96-9295-45a04be167d9",
                                                "text": "What mighty and inviſible Remora is this in Matrimony able to demurre, and to contemne all the divorſive engines in heaven or earth."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "86dfd9c1-9ec4-4236-bbd2-264f4d6984e5",
                                                "sense_id": "6340fcd6-dbe2-3c96-9295-45a04be167d9",
                                                "text": "Increased tone of the muscular fibres of the middle coat of the intestines may not infrequently give rise to a remora in the passage of their contents, and the effect of many of that class of medicines called astringents appears to be owing to their tonic powers."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "980c890b-db9a-4591-af29-8a61fa40f6db",
                                                "sense_id": "6340fcd6-dbe2-3c96-9295-45a04be167d9",
                                                "text": "The local remoræ of blood which occur in cholera infantum here, will not bear, as a general practice, the abstraction of blood for their relief; they are more under the control of revellent remedies, not of a depletive kind."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b35120ae-a89e-3cfc-aba4-be45b4ee6dc7",
                                        "entry_id": "d767bf05-f129-368c-b975-1a16ed319518",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A surgical instrument, intended to retain parts in their places."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9ac5d764-ee83-4f6b-bd6e-73d9e862a574",
                                                "sense_id": "b35120ae-a89e-3cfc-aba4-be45b4ee6dc7",
                                                "text": "Rem′ora [...] The name of two surgical instruments, intended to retain parts in situ. The one was used, formerly, in castration, to prevent the intestines from protruding at the inguinal ring: the other, called Rem′ora Hilda′ni, (F[rench]) Arrêt d'Hildan, εχενηις, was employed to retain fractures and luxations reduced. It is not now used."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
